  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nTonight through Sunday:\n\nAs of this writing, what had been a bank of extensive fog and\nvery low clouds across Chicago's far northern suburbs this\nmorning has finally contracted to a remaining patch near\nWinthrop Harbor, IL. With lake enhanced moisture evident in the\nWaukegan and southeast WI dew point obs, wouldn't be surprised \nif patchy fog sneaks back into northeastern Lake County this\nevening from continued light to moderate southeasterly boundary\nlayer flow.\n\nAnother calm night is in store tonight, with the main change \nhappening aloft in terms of strengthening southerly flow just \nabove the nocturnal inversion. Over the southeast half or third \nof the area (I-88 corridor in interior Chicago metro and points \nsoutheast), flow aloft should be sufficiently weak to enable \npatchy shallow ground fog development. Can't completely rule out\nvery localized dense fog, but the setup isn't nearly as \nfavorable as this (Friday) morning. High clouds will filter in \nfrom the northwest overnight into Saturday morning, keeping\novernight lows a bit milder across interior northern Illinois in\nparticular.\n\nOn Saturday, high temperatures will likely be close to a carbon\ncopy of today (Friday), in the lower to mid 70s. Illinois side\nlake cooling will be confined to the immediate shoreline during\nthe afternoon as southerly winds back to southeast with lake\ninfluence. Following a calm and cool (relative to Saturday \nafternoon's warmth) Saturday night, Sunday will likely be the \nwarmest day of this stretch unless mid and high cloud cover \nlimits high temps a bit.\n\nSunday Night through Friday:\n\nA backdoor cold front will move across the area Sunday night,\nknocking temps down vs. the weekend for Monday's highs, but \nstill above average for late October. The coolest readings \n(around 60F/low 60s) will be found near Lake Michigan due to \nstrengthening north-northeast winds. Another period of well \nabove average temps will follow through Wednesday or Thursday, \nfollowed by modest cooling to close out the week in the wake of \na cold frontal passage. Chances for rain mid to late next week, \nwhile not zero, are low/nebulous, and the best chance for \nanything of note looks to be confined to well west and northwest\nof the local area.\n\nCastro",
